export const AUTHORING_GUIDE_MARKDOWN = "# RPGCode program authoring\n\n**TOOL-10 Program editing (story 11.7, #1705; editor-features T-40).** *In plain terms:* an author can open a `.prg`, see it coloured as they type, jump to its functions, labels and comments, see the shape of the command they are typing, insert a command from a list, start a new program from a header the game chooses, and hide everything but the text. It works the same in the browser editor (which shows the text in Monaco, [ADR 0021](../../adr/0021-monaco-code-editor.md)) and in VS Code (which shows it in its own text editor). None of it needs a syntax grammar from the later grammar epic, and if the language server fails the author can still type and save. *Precisely:* this entry fixes the pieces below; each is shared by both hosts where the host has the part, and no host keeps its own copy of the rules.\n- **Navigation (bookmarks).** `textDocument/documentSymbol` lists, in source order and as one flat list, the functions, classes, labels and comments of the open file. A **comment bookmark** is a comment that starts its line (the first non-blank text is `//`, `**`, `*` or `/*`); its name is the comment's text without the marker and without a closing `*/`, trimmed, and a comment with no letter or digit in it (a ruled line of `-----`) is not listed. A trailing comment after code is not listed. A block comment is listed once, by its first line. Symbols are reported with kind `Function`, `Class`, `Key` (a label) and `String` (a comment). The browser editor shows them as its bookmark list and VS Code shows them in the Outline.\n- **Call tips** are [the signature help above](#tool-1); the browser editor shows them in Monaco's parameter hints, VS Code in its own.\n- **Command reference and insertion.** The quick reference is a projection of the registered commands' documentation (`commandDocumentation`), computed by `editor-model` from the same catalogue as hover, and so needs no running language server. One entry per public name and alias: `name`, `canonicalName`, `aliasOf` (or `null`), `syntax` (the call as it is written, such as `mwin(text)`, with an optional parameter in brackets, a by-reference parameter prefixed `&` and a variadic one ending `...`), the one-sentence `summary`, and `groups`. **Insert** puts `name(` and the names of the **required** parameters, separated by `, `, and `)` at the cursor, replacing any selection; the editor selects the parameter text so the author overtypes it. A command with no required parameters inserts `name()` with the cursor between the brackets. Double-clicking an entry (the browser) or choosing it in the picker (VS Code) inserts it. Insertion is one undoable edit.\n- **Header template.** The preference `program.headerTemplate` (a string, user or project layer, at most 500 characters) is the text a new program starts with. `{file}` becomes the file name (`intro.prg`), `{name}` the file name without its extension, and `{path}` the project-relative path; any other `{…}` is left as written, so a template can never fail. The default is `// {file}` followed by a blank line. **New program** expands the template and opens the created file. It refuses a selected path when its case-insensitive existence check detects an existing file, with an “already exists” message, and creates nothing. A host **MUST** use a genuine atomic exclusive-create operation when its API supports one. The current browser and VS Code adapters instead check the directory listing and then call an ordinary write: an external actor can create the path between those steps, and that write may replace the concurrent file. This disclosed check/write race is accepted for those hosts; it is not an absolute no-overwrite guarantee. See [ADR 0035](../../adr/0035-program-new-no-overwrite-limits.md).\n- **Distraction-free mode** (the legacy \"Notepad mode\"). A command that hides the editor's panels, tab strip and toolbars and leaves only the text, and that a second use or Escape reverses. The browser editor hides its shell panels and the program toolbar; VS Code uses its Zen mode. Neither mode changes the document, the dirty state or the language service.\n- **Failure.** The language server is a helper to the text, never its owner. If it is missing, fails to start, stops, or answers an error, the document still edits, undoes, saves and reopens; the editor says in one status line that language features are unavailable, and the features that need the server (colouring, bookmarks, call tips, hover, diagnostics) simply show nothing. Header insertion, command insertion, undo and save do not use the server.\n- **VS Code extensions.** The language client stays in `extensions/rpgcode` ([TOOL-2](#tool-2)); this entry is the editor epic's answer to its question whether the editor host takes the client over, and the answer is no: two extensions keep two jobs. `extensions/rpgcode` turns semantic highlighting on for the `rpgcode` language and is otherwise unchanged. The editor extension (`tk-reawakened.editor`) adds the commands **New program**, **Insert header**, **Insert command** (a picker over the quick reference) and **Distraction-free mode**; they read the shared preferences and the shared command catalogue, never the language server, so they work when it is absent. The settings of the header template live with the other editor preferences.\n\nThis guide is drawn from the repository's TOOL-10 editor specification. It is a maintained authoring reference, not the legacy HTML manual.";
export const SAVING_HELP_MARKDOWN = "# Saving and external file changes\n\nUse **Save active editor** or **Ctrl+S** (**Cmd+S** on macOS) to save the editor you are working in. **Save All Open Documents** saves all open documents with unsaved changes. The editor shows save failures and conflicts where you can resolve them.\n\n## Changes made by another program\n\nSome hosts can watch project files and report changes while you work. When file watching is unavailable, external changes are checked when you save instead.\n\nBefore writing, the editor compares the file with the version it last read or saved. If it finds a change, it stops the save and shows a conflict. Your edits remain available while you choose how to resolve it. The choices depend on the host: keeping your edits and reloading the disk version are offered when supported.\n\nThe comparison and write are separate operations. A change another program makes after the comparison but before the write can still be replaced. This is a [known save limitation](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/issues/2207), not a guarantee that simultaneous writes are safe.\n";
export const WHATS_NEW_MARKDOWN = "# What's New\n\n- Follow a placed item's shared graphics and local activation/parallel programs, edit linked ANM/TBM artwork, and return to the same board slot (#1723; [connected authoring](docs/editor-features.md#p0-connected-authoring)). Explicit Save preserves the opened formats and unrelated data. Nested-game previews find their own assets and refresh when a retained view is reactivated; linked bitmaps paint on opening. Board tests execute programs from the captured saved files, and neither board nor normal-game runs hot-reload later edits (#3040, #3111, #3114, #3121).\n\n- Shared editor controls now use readable light, dark and native high-contrast roles, with distinct selection/focus markers, legible read-only fields, wrapping header/status rows and usable narrow program panels (#2988; [styling and host ownership](docs/design/editor-style-delivery.md)). Preferences and New board use bounded, readable dialogs; expanded shelves keep a visible marker. Opening a browser project reports pending work, blocks competing opens and recovers after a real read failure. Existing document commands, authored artwork and native VS Code text/preferences keep their owners.\n\n- Draw vectors and program regions, place item sprites and layer images, and edit their properties on the board canvas (#1721; [P0 object tools](docs/editor-features.md#p0-board-objects)). Completed gestures share the board's undo history; numeric inspectors, linked item/program visits and explicit slot removal preserve untouched records, empty slots and the opened file version. Drafts survive mode changes, native confirmation owns Escape, and picked transparency stays staged until Apply. Game-folder board previews resolve their own assets, and unkeyed sprite frames retain opaque white (#3029, #3030, #3033, #3038).\n\n- Edit board settings and links, follow linked boards or programs and return to the same selection, and place the player start by mouse or keyboard (#1714; T-27/T-29). Edits are undoable and survive saving and reopening in the browser and VS Code. “Set as initial board” asks before changing the owning game file; normal board saves never change it. Unsupported or lossy values are refused without discarding the draft.\n\n- Run the game, a board test or a program test from VS Code's **Run and Debug** view (#2919; [ADR 0037](docs/adr/0037-vscode-launches-in-run-and-debug.md)). The editor extension adds the `tkr` debug type: a `launch.json` configuration uses exactly the fields of the platform-services launch: `\"kind\": \"game\"`, `\"board\"` or `\"program\"`, then `main`, `projectFolder` and `seed`, a board test's `start` and `party`, and a program test's `program` and `fixture` (font, font size, message-window graphic, party and start). Press F5 to start it; any value left out is asked for with the same questions as the Run Game and Test Program commands, so an empty configuration works. It saves first, runs from a snapshot in the same Running Game tab, and shows the snapshot, run state and diagnostics in the Debug Console. The debug toolbar's Stop ends the run and Restart saves again and starts a new snapshot; closing the tab ends the session. Breakpoints in the running game are not supported yet (#2920); the commands keep working.\n\n- Run saved RPGCode creator tests from the browser workbench's Run panel or VS Code's **RPG Toolkit: Run saved tests** command (#1707; [creator tests](docs/spec/engine/rpgcode-tooling.md#tool-5-creator-api)). The editor saves accepted edits first, runs the same real-engine framework as the command line, and shows saved revisions, fixture frames, assertion results and links to their owning source. Tests can use a seed and case-name filter; edits that race the saved-file read refuse the run rather than silently testing older bytes. Test-only files belong under `.tkr-tests/` and are excluded from game packages.\n\n- Scene-only checkpoints retain full audio and action data, historical baked canvases and held frames while resources rehydrate. Constructor asset requests are delivered once, and resumed ticks and drawing match uninterrupted execution without changing ordinary state hashes or game-save formats (#2831).\n\n- Effect-observer regressions now cover actual bounded-log eviction and draining with exact production sequences, alongside real native-command observation (#2850).\n\n- The native NewProject acceptance proof navigates directly to its existing destination in the real folder picker, without depending on a remembered parent listing; it still verifies generated backing files and automatic editor opening (#2858).\n\n- No-skips Git fixtures isolate inherited repository, signing and global configuration, then restore the original environment after each synchronous fixture (#2852).\n\n- The native web-workbench proof loads the real RPGCode companion extension, builds it in CI, and refuses a missing browser bundle instead of leaving programs in Plain Text (#2844).\n\n- Story 12.7 adds shared binary `.anm` creation and editing in browser and VS Code: ordered image staging, multi-frame 4×4 tile compositions with template reuse, frame metadata, playback, and normal save/undo. Persistent SHA-256 checkpoint groups retain original and intended files before wizard writes, report partial failures, retry remaining work, and make restores recoverable across reopening. Text minor 0 remains read-only; general history retention and timelines remain under #1828 (#1718, #2805, #2813–#2816).\n\n[Read the full changelog](https://github.com/tk-reawakened/rpgtoolkit-reawakened/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md).";
